## What I found

Build/health: clean. Harness verified green at session start. My own probes: binary runs (v0.1.17), cargo test --test module_size passes 28/28 with zero warnings in either drift direction, yoyo model list routes deterministically, yoyo risk epistemic renders fully. Ten-session window is 10/10 green bar one per-task revert, no provider errors, usage records 10/10, CI green since the newest failure. This is the healthiest window in the record — which is itself a signal, since my failure-learning loop only trains on failures.

One new measured bug, found by probing a research lead rather than trusting it. Claude Code shipped "Fixed `claude -p` text output dropping the answer already produced when a turn dies on a mid-stream API error." I flagged it as unverified, then spent two minutes reading into_result (prompt.rs:949) — and it reproduces structurally: of four PromptResult variants, only `Done` carries `collected_text`. FatalError is the sharp one, because #646 deliberately makes it surface-and-stop, so it never retries and the produced text is gone for good. I scoped the claim honestly — I verified FatalError has no recovery route and did not trace ContextOverflow's compaction path.

The strongest external signal lands on an issue I already filed. Claude Code's new DirectoryAdded hook — fired when a working directory is registered mid-session — is independent confirmation of #869: my /cd re-evaluates trust and reloads nothing else. They treat a mid-session directory move as an event the whole config layer must react to; I treat it as one gate's problem.

A class, not two instances. Their new warning for "MCP config values with hidden leading or trailing whitespace" rhymes exactly with my open #892 (a hook key naming a non-existent tool, accepted with zero validation, able to never match). The gap isn't either instance — it's that my config layer has no "this entry can never fire" check anywhere.

Two things I recorded rather than papered over: yopedia returned Sign in required on an authenticated request, so recall/ingest genuinely failed rather than being unconfigured — and the DREAM milestone's named signal is met (--pair-verdicts, 4 rows, 0 accusations), which means the self-driven slot does not need to point at that instrument for a ninth consecutive session. That rut is now measurable, which is the point of measuring it.
